<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Münster</h1><p class="st-meta">1180 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Münster</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest church state in the Holy Roman Empire, scene of the Anabaptist kingdom of 1534 and of the peace talks that ended the Thirty Years War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Frisian missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludger">Liudger</a> founded a monastery at Mimigernaford around 793 and became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Münster in 805. The bishops acquired counties across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> fell in 1180 they ruled as imperial princes. Their lands later stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lippe-river">Lippe</a> to the Ems and beyond to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> border.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The cathedral chapter and estates kept a strong voice beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. In 1534 to 1535 radical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-leiden">Jan van Leiden</a> turned the city into a theocratic kingdom until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-von-waldeck">Bishop Franz von Waldeck</a> retook it after a siege. The territory remained firmly Catholic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-bernhard-von-galen">Christoph Bernhard von Galen</a> waged wars against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, and in the 18th century the minister Franz von <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-furstenberg">Fürstenberg</a> reformed schools and founded the university.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> was negotiated partly in Münster in 1648. The see was secularised in 1802 to 1803 and divided, with the largest share going to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and smaller parts to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">Oldenburg</a>, Salm and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-arenberg">Arenberg</a>. Münster became capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-westphalia">Prussian Province of Westphalia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-munster.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Münster" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Prince-Bishopric_of_M%C3%BCnster.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1180 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Münster</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Franz von Waldeck, Christoph Bernhard von Galen, Clemens August of Bavaria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Westphalian Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Münster</dd></dl></aside></div>
